WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.043
Bacteria are tiny living cells. In a lab, they are grown on nutrient agar, a jelly with food in it. It sits in a Petri dish, a shallow round dish with a lid. The bacteria reproduce until they form a cloudy layer all over the agar.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:34.520
An antibiotic is a drug that kills bacteria, or stops them reproducing. In a disc test, a paper disc is soaked in antibiotic and put on the agar. The antibiotic diffuses, or spreads out, into the agar. Where it kills the bacteria, a clear zone forms.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:56.770
Step one: compare the clear zones. Disc A has the largest zone, so antibiotic A is the most effective. Disc B has a small zone. Disc C has no zone at all. The bacteria are resistant to C, which means C does not affect them. This is only fair if the discs, the concentrations and the time are the same.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:19.345
Some questions show a growth graph instead. This made-up example shows the number of bacteria over time. With no antibiotic, the number rises. If the line stays level, the antibiotic is bacteriostatic: the bacteria stop reproducing, but stay alive. If the line falls, it is bactericidal: the bacteria are killed.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:39.015
Step three: use the control. A control shows what happens without the antibiotic, for example a disc in water only. A dish with no bacteria added can still grow a few, from the air, or from a sample being tested, such as milk. Also check how many bacteria were added at the start.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:56.816
Aseptic technique means working so that no unwanted microbes get in, for example a sterile loop and a flame. Tape the lid, but do not seal it all round. Incubate at twenty-five degrees Celsius or below, so that bacteria which cause human disease do not grow.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:13.182
Now a real exam question. The method tests antibiotic X against antibiotic Y. The diagram shows the dish after three days. The question asks you to explain which antibiotic is more effective. Three marks.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:23.798
X is more effective than Y. But the choice alone earns no mark. Point one: the clear zone around X is larger than the clear zone around Y.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:36.211
Point two: the antibiotic diffused out of the paper disc into the agar. Point three: in the clear zone, the bacteria have been killed. So X killed bacteria over a larger area.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.325
Here is the full answer. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:53.199
A common mistake is to choose the smaller clear zone, or to say bacteria grow best in the clear zone. The clear zone is where bacteria have been killed.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:03:03.862
Another mistake is to say a level line on a graph means the bacteria are killed. Level means they stopped reproducing. Only a falling line shows killing.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:13.751
Last, do not just repeat numbers from a table. Explain the difference, for example more bacteria at the start, or bacteria already in the milk.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:36.681
Your turn. A doctor tests three antibiotics against bacteria from a patient's throat. These are made-up results. There are three discs. The clear zones are: P, 22 millimetres. Q, 0 millimetres. R, 9 millimetres. Which antibiotic should the doctor give? Explain. What does the result for Q show? Pause here and work it out.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:48.851
Choose P. Its clear zone is the largest, 22 millimetres. P diffused out of the disc and killed bacteria over the largest area, so it is the most effective.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:55.317
Q has no clear zone. So the bacteria are resistant to Q. It would not help this patient.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:09.276
So, every time: make your choice. Quote the evidence, such as the larger clear zone or a falling line. Then explain it: the antibiotic diffused out and killed bacteria, or stopped them reproducing.
